WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.310
How can a country overcome a crisis?

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:05.890
How can it reinvent itself, change, transform?

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:09.910
These are two of the questions you asked us
in the comments section, here on VisualPolitik.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:15.730
Well, my friends, I think we can say that
the economy usually evolves through cycles.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:20.100
That is, a growth phase is sooner or later
always followed by a crisis, and then this

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.660
crisis is followed by a new growth phase.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.660
And so on….

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:35.230
For example, since World War II, the US has
undergone 11 complete economic cycles, that

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.570
is, both growth and crisis years.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.550
However, my friends...

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.679
Things aren’t always that simple…

00:00:42.679 --> 00:00:43.679
sometimes…

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:45.850
leaving a crisis is very, very hard.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.850
So much so that decades could go by;

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:54.910
so much so, that even a rich and prosperous
country can... stop being one.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:02.260
Because don’t think that this only affects
emerging countries, not at all.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:07.420
Even the richest countries can suffer this
type of deep crisis… usually due to political

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.300
reasons, or rather, bad policies.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.969
But, yes, my friends, a country can even escape
these deepest of crises.

00:01:14.969 --> 00:01:21.909
We saw it here on VISUALPOLITIK with, for
example, the case of New Zealand, which after

00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:28.700
undergoing a very harsh crisis managed to
completely overturn their situation.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:34.780
Well... in this video we’ll show another
example... that of Canada!

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.869
Yes, you heard me right.

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:41.600
Did you know that Canada almost went bankrupt?

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:49.659
Well, listen up.

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:54.590
Canada is one of the richest, freest, most
prosperous countries with the highest quality

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.440
of life on the planet.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.990
It doesn’t even matter what ranking we use:

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.909
It can be the United Nations HDI, the Corruption
Perception Index of Transparency International,

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:11.000
the Fraser Institute’s Human Freedom Index
or the Legatum Prosperity Index.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.811
In all of them, Canada is always among the
highest on the list...

00:02:15.811 --> 00:02:17.350
But this video isn’t about rankings...

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:21.220
so... just one more.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:26.970
See, in the latest ranking of the best countries
prepared by US News magazine and the Wharton

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:33.170
School, Canada appears as the second best
country to live in the world.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:41.280
So, it’s pretty clear, as we already saw
on this channel, Canada, dear viewers, is

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.200
very popular right now.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.500
However, things in Canada weren’t always
this happy.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:55.360
There was a time, in the 60s and especially
in the 70s, where everything was about to

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.760
collapse.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:05.560
(THE DISASTER)

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.530
For years, Canada was very similar to the
United States, it was practically its twin

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.820
brother:

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:17.340
It had a very small government, the country
was very decentralized and taxes were very

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.480
similar… of course, the idea then was to
compete to attract investment and thus, also,

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.930
people from abroad, immigrants.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.490
However, this all changed at the end of the
60s.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:32.950
At that time Canada sought to distance itself
from the United States and to try a new political

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:34.420
and economic model.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:39.680
The great architect of this "revolution" was,
Pierre Trudeau, yes, the father of the current

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.870
Prime Minister, Justin Trudeau.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:53.690
Of course...

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.550
father and son’s politics are by no means
the same.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.140
It looks like Justin Trudeau did his homework.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:04.030
But... let's not stray from the subject too
much.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:11.790
The fact is that Trudeau’s father governed
Canada virtually uninterrupted, between 1968

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.110
and 1984...

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:18.940
And the fact is that throughout these 16 years
in power the country changed a lot.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:24.380
See, Trudeau wanted to implement a new political
model in Canada, which was a bit different

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:29.610
from Washington’s capitalism and somewhat
closer to Soviet Moscow’s collectivism.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.910
(“As against the "invisible hand" of Adam
Smith, there has to be a visible hand of politicians

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:39.100
whose objective is to have the kind of society
that is caring and humane”.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.100
Pierre Trudeau.)

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:43.230
In fact, in international politics, one of
the things the prime minister was best-known

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.660
for was his close friendship with Fidel Castro.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:55.470
In any case, the truth is that we could say
that Trudeau's father went a bit far:

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.740
he raised taxes a lot, increased public spending,
nationalized companies and because he thought

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:05.160
that all international trade and investment
was bad, due to competition... he decided

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.560
to set up barriers

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.380
UMMM... barriers?

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:16.870
Who does that sound like?

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.580
If that weren’t enough, he also made the
Central Bank print banknotes to spare, to

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.420
expand credit and finance the government.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:31.310
Does this recipe sound familiar?

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:41.470
Well, the result of all this, my friends,
is that within a few years, the deficit, unemployment

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:45.260
and inflation rates put the whole country
up against the ropes.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:50.590
So, of course, when in 1984 Pierre Trudeau
left office, he did so with the lowest popularity

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:54.160
level for a Canadian prime minister in decades.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.889
Only 2 out of 10 Canadians still supported
him

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:04.669
And it was then that Canadian citizens had
to make a very important decision.

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:12.419
(THE CHANGE)

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:16.910
In the September 1984 elections, the Conservatives
won an overwhelming victory.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.680
It was clear that the country could not continue
along Trudeau’s path.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.290
The new Prime Minister, Brian Mulroney, decided
to reverse the situation and began to strengthen

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:27.090
ties with President Ronald Reagan.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:31.040
A new era had begun...

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:37.630
Over the next 10 years the Conservative government
did many things to improve Canada's economy:

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:42.310
For example, it lowered taxes, negotiated
NAFTA, the North American Free Trade Agreement

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.570
that unites Canada, the US and Mexico,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:49.330
And following in Margaret Thatcher’s steps,
launched an ambitious plan to privatize large

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:52.390
public companies, such as Air Canada and Petro-Canada.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.790
However, dear viewers, he didn’t do one
thing…

00:06:55.790 --> 00:07:00.900
the most difficult and unpopular action: control
public spending.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.800
In 1990, the state held more than half of
the economy, taxes were still very high, there

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.730
was a huge public deficit, and public debt
rose like foam.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:15.180
Conservatives had taken the easiest measures…
but at the moment of truth they hadn’t really

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.430
shown much commitment.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:20.620
Therefore, in the following 1993 elections,
Pierre Trudeau’s leftist Liberal party won

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:25.139
the elections again... this time, promising
a true revolution.

00:07:25.139 --> 00:07:33.110
Could Pierre Trudeau's party save Canada?

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:37.190
Given his background, you can’t deny this
would’ve have been very difficult to imagine.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:38.190
But...

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.760
my friends, everything was different this
time.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:45.530
The new government wanted to completely change
the country’s model once and for all, and

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:49.639
no, they no longer wanted to know anything
about Moscow or the Soviet model.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:54.370
The main idea was that quality of life wasn’t
only related to the economy, it depended on

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:55.370
it.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.870
If they wanted to achieve a high quality of
life they needed to strengthen Canada’s

00:07:58.870 --> 00:07:59.870
economy.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:04.080
And that’s what they did, especially under
the Minister of Finance, Paul Martin, the

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.030
true change promoter.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:11.389
Well, the fact is that within the first years,
this leftist government launched a sharp public

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:15.590
spending cut, elimited many unnecessary programs
and adjusted spending.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:20.740
For example, they strongly reduced military
spending and many companies’ subsidies.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:25.800
In just two years, in real terms, spending
dropped by 15%.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:29.070
Under the principle which states that the
State shouldn’t do things that companies

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:34.129
can do better, the government decided to continue
with the privatization plan... in which they

00:08:34.129 --> 00:08:38.499
went as far as privatizing the railways and
even the aviation management system.

00:08:38.499 --> 00:08:44.529
And, no, planes in Canada haven’t fallen
nor have there been more accidents than in

00:08:44.529 --> 00:08:45.529
other countries.

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:49.709
On the contrary, Canada’s aviation management
system is considered one of the best in the

00:08:49.709 --> 00:08:50.709
world.

00:08:50.709 --> 00:08:53.459
The fact is that in just 4 years, the situation
was under control.

00:08:53.459 --> 00:08:58.029
The government had gone from having a huge
deficit to a surplus.

00:08:58.029 --> 00:09:04.170
Check out this graphic.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.589
Of course, as you can imagine, these measures
weren’t easy, but one thing seemed to be

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:14.689
very clear to Canadians, so much so that in
the 1997 elections, the liberal, leftist party,

00:09:14.689 --> 00:09:19.670
won again, by promising still more careful
government spending and, on this occasion,

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:20.670
fewer taxes.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:23.860
As they said, my friends, their role was to
protect and give opportunities to the most

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:27.689
disenfranchised, not to waste everyone's money.

00:09:27.689 --> 00:09:28.689
It seems...

00:09:28.689 --> 00:09:29.689
sensible.

00:09:29.689 --> 00:09:30.689
Don’t you think?

00:09:30.689 --> 00:09:34.430
Listen to Paul Martin’s words in 2003, as
he reviewed their 10 years in power:

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:38.249
(“We have to build a 21st century economy
in Canada for Canadians.

00:09:38.249 --> 00:09:42.439
We succeeded in the last 10 years because
we did not deviate from our course – balanced

00:09:42.439 --> 00:09:45.999
budgets, a continually dropping debt ratio,
lower taxes.

00:09:45.999 --> 00:09:47.679
We must stay on that course.

00:09:47.679 --> 00:09:50.110
[…] We must be a government committed to
accountability.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:53.570
A government that treats taxpayers’ money
like it is your money”.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:54.570
Paul Martin)

00:09:54.570 --> 00:10:05.389
My friends, this revolution was a real turning
point in Canada’s history.

00:10:05.389 --> 00:10:13.249
The change that the country experienced in
the 80s and especially in the 90s... allowed

00:10:13.249 --> 00:10:16.490
for this country’s situation to change completely.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:20.810
Canada went from being considered an honorary
member of the "third world" by the Wall Street

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:25.269
Journal, due to its public debt problems...
to becoming the most fashionable country.

00:10:25.269 --> 00:10:34.309
The liberal party hadn’t only solved the
country’s problems, they had changed its

00:10:34.309 --> 00:10:35.309
model.

00:10:35.309 --> 00:10:39.309
In the following years, up until today, all
political options opt for the Canadian model.

00:10:39.309 --> 00:10:43.759
Mind you, Canada isn’t a tax haven, far
from it, but they have understood that companies

00:10:43.759 --> 00:10:46.730
need to be cared for and not punished.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.369
Check this out:

00:10:48.369 --> 00:10:53.550
You heard that right.

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:57.189
If we take all taxes that companies actually
pay into account, this country’s situation

00:10:57.189 --> 00:10:58.589
is very advantageous.

00:10:58.589 --> 00:11:02.949
In fact, Canada is one of the few developed
countries that has been decreasing its taxes

00:11:02.949 --> 00:11:06.220
from time to time, for almost 20 years, to
make the country more competitive...

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:15.089
And look at how, for example, company taxes
have evolved:

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:17.600
And this policy has mostly favored the weakest.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.980
Salaries have grown a lot, this is one of
the countries that receives the most immigrants,

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.429
and employment rates are very high.

00:11:24.429 --> 00:11:30.050
In addition, if all this weren’t enough,
in recent years Canada has also become a world

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:35.699
civil liberties champion, as we saw here at
VISUALPOLITIK in a previousvideo.

00:11:35.699 --> 00:11:39.809
And this, dear viewers, is how Canada managed
to escape its greatest crisis.

00:11:39.809 --> 00:11:43.660
And by the way, just as it happened in New
Zealand and Australia, these reforms were

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.809
carried out by leftist parties.

00:11:45.809 --> 00:11:50.619
But this isn’t about left or right, it’s
about understanding that a strong, competitive

00:11:50.619 --> 00:11:55.319
and globally open economy is the best formula
to improve everyone’s quality of life.

00:11:55.319 --> 00:11:57.220
But, having said that, now it’s your turn.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:59.730
Do you think Canada is a good role model?

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:03.759
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in this survey.

00:12:03.759 --> 00:12:08.389
I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did and don’t forget to

00:12:08.389 --> 00:12:11.389
subscribe to our channel for brand new videos
every Monday and Thursday.

00:12:11.389 --> 00:12:14.759
Also, don’t forget to check out our friends
at the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:12:14.759 --> 00:12:18.529
the vocals in this episode that were not mine!

00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:19.170
And as always, thanks for watching!

